course: dictionary
lesson: 3033
title: Words for people in power
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words name a rank, which is a position of power. Look at the clues around the word. «The lesson is about William the Conqueror, the English accepting him, and Edwin and Morcar, the earls of Mercia and Northumbria; earls were powerful lords.» The sentence tells you straight away that earls were powerful lords.
explain: An earl is a British nobleman, a man of high rank. In the ranks from top to bottom, an earl comes below a marquess and above a viscount. So a duke is higher than an earl, and a baron is lower.
explain: In early England, an earl was a powerful lord who ruled a large area for the king. Think of him as the king's helper, looking after a big part of the land. The sentence names two earls, Edwin and Morcar, who each ruled a region.
fact: An earl is a British nobleman of high rank.
fact: The ranks from top to bottom are: duke, marquess, earl, viscount, baron.
fact: The plural of earl is earls, as in «the earls of Mercia and Northumbria».
fact: In early England, an earl was a powerful lord who ruled a large area for the king.
fact: An earl's wife is called a countess.
fact: Earl is also a man's name, so check the sentence to see which meaning is used.
exercise: «The lesson is about William the Conqueror, the English accepting him, and Edwin and Morcar, the earls of Mercia and Northumbria.» What were the earls? | Servants of the church | Kings of the Vikings | *Powerful lords | Castle builders
exercise: Which rank comes just above an earl? | *Marquess | Viscount | Baron
exercise: What is a countess? | *An earl's wife | A castle on a hill | A king's daughter
test: Which rank is lower than an earl? | *Viscount | Duke | Marquess
test: In early England, what did an earl do for the king? | Built churches | *Ruled a large area for the king | Sailed ships
exam: Which list puts the ranks in the right order from top to bottom? | *Duke, marquess, earl, viscount, baron | Viscount, earl, duke, baron | Earl, duke, baron, marquess | Baron, viscount, earl, duke
